+            <p>A few months ago, I left my biggest personal project. When I came back, I realized I no longer understood my own backend.</p><p>Originally, around 90% of the backend code was written by me (very little AI usage). After leaving the project untouched for five months, I came back and realized I had no idea where to continue. Even my Git history couldn't help. TBH the problem wasn't AI. It started much earlier: I had no roadmap, no milestones, and development became completely random.</p><p>So what did I do? I started using AI to resume the development of the project. At first, it looked handy, making views and DB models with only a simple prompt, I told myself: MAN! I was in denial for not using Claude and for writing most of the code myself at first!</p><p>Then a problem became vivid: too many views and DB models were scripted with little review from my side, a mistake I admit and regret too much. I told myself it is time to stop being lazy and review the code at that stage. But every view depended on several functions, models, and utilities. Reviewing a single endpoint meant tracing code across the project. Django, the framework I'd been using for years, suddenly felt intimidating.</p><p>If AI made that mess then AI can understand that mess, right? That was what I told myself. And I was wrong. AI started misunderstanding the code and even edited the code blocks it generated at first. I would be talking to Claude on 2 different chats and the answers would be different. Honestly, I would not blame any AI tool for that, a clear roadmap or set of actions was not there, AI was just improvising.</p><p>Then I lost hope in the whole thing, the project I thought would be the biggest in my portfolio was over, it felt like all the effort was wasted! I tried to fix as much as I could, but procrastination kicked in. For sometime, I did not open VS Code just because the code looked INTIMIDATING. AI did not ruin the project, it just accelerated the consequences of my plan.</p><p>And here comes the role of this series, by sharing updates as I go, I will force myself to improve the code and eventually reclaim the codebase. These posts will be a commitment device, as James Clear said in his book "Atomic Habits" (amazing book btw, recommend you read it).</p><p>So as for today (August 6, 2026), the project stands on commit <a class="post-inline-link" href="https://github.com/Omar-Shaker-Elbana/Store_Hub/commit/87bac7e5a90045af0c0aa3b9a6dd0c67f5cf7664" rel="noopener" target="_blank">#102</a>. <a class="post-inline-link" href="https://github.com/Omar-Shaker-Elbana/Store_Hub" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Full repo</a></p><p>Every couple of days, I'll refactor one piece of the backend, explain what was wrong, what I learned, and how I fixed it. Let's see if I can reclaim this codebase!</p>
